You are describing one image from a Telegram sticker pack, to be indexed in a sticker search database. The uniform light-gray background replaces transparency - never describe it as a surface, room, or sky.

Write EXACTLY these labeled lines, one per line, in this order. Each line is one short sentence or phrase. No markdown, no intro, no "This sticker shows...".

SUMMARY: <the whole scene in 5-15 words>
CHARACTER: <who is shown: generic species/person ("gray cartoon cat", "bearded man", "chibi girl") or the well-known character's name ONLY if the design makes it unmistakable>
APPEARANCE: <colors, clothing, fur, accessories, distinguishing features that help tell characters apart>
ACTION: <what the character is physically doing; if nothing notable, write "none">
EMOTION: <feeling shown by face and body language; if ambiguous, write "unclear">
OBJECTS: <inanimate props in the picture; if none, write "none">
STYLE: <art style in 1-3 words: "pixel art", "flat vector", "watercolor", "3d render", "chibi", "sketch" - pick what you see>
TEXT: <transcribe any text drawn in the image exactly, or "none">
WHEN TO USE: <situations this sticker fits as a reply, based ONLY on what is visible ("reply to good news", "shut down an argument", "saying thanks")>

Rules:
- Ground every word in what is actually visible. This feeds a search index, so precision matters more than flattery.
- YCH: if one of the characters is rendered as a flat, featureless figure of a single solid color - fully WHITE or fully LIGHT BLUE, with no fur markings, clothing or shading - treat it as a YCH placeholder ("your character here"), whether or not any "YCH" text is visible. Then write "YCH" in SUMMARY, name it in APPEARANCE (e.g. "solid white YCH placeholder"), and describe the pose it offers. A normally drawn character that merely has white or blue fur is NOT a YCH - the giveaway is the featureless solid-color body.
- Do NOT guess names of real people, brands, franchises or character names unless the identity is obvious and certain; otherwise describe by appearance.
- Do NOT invent actions, emotions, or objects that are not visible. If something cannot be determined, write "unclear" - "unclear" is always better than a guess.
- Do not speculate about hidden context, backstory, or the artist's intent.
- Ignore image borders, watermarks and compression artifacts unless they carry meaningful drawn text.
- Use plain declarative English, no hashtags.
